---

Poke the @clove{1} into the onion. Into a #small saucepan{} place the @milk{1%litre}, @onion{0.5}, @bay leaf{1}, and @salt{pinch}. Heat gently to a simmer. Take off the heat and leave to infuse for ~{15%minutes}, then remove the onion and bay leaf.

Melt @butter{50%g} in a #medium saucepan{} over a medium-low heat, and stir in the @flour{50%g}. Cook for ~{2%minutes} until it smells biscuity, then add the milk, one ladle at a time, stirring it in to produce a smooth sauce. Turn the heat right down and leave to simmer for 15 to ~{20%minutes} until thick.

Meanwhile, bring a #large pan{} of well-salted water to the boil. Cook the @cauliflower{1%medium}(cut into florets) for ~{4%minutes}, until just tender. Drain thoroughly and keep warm.

Melt most of the remaining @butter{25%g} in a #frying pan{} over a medium-high heat and fry the cauliflower until slightly browned and caramelised. Season and spoon into a #baking dish{}, then put the rest of the butter into the pan, add the breadcrumbs and fry until crunchy and golden. Season with @salt and @pepper. Preheat the grill to medium-high.

Stir the @Lancashire cheese{100%g}(grated) into the sauce until it melts, and then add the @double cream{75%ml}, @nutmeg{}(grated), and season to taste.

Pour the sauce over the cauliflower. Top with the @cheddar cheese{25%g}(grated), followed by the @stale white bread{4%slices}(in breadcrumbs). Grill until golden and bubbling. Do no let the breadcrumbs burn. Alternatively use the oven.

> Try Jamie Oliver's version next time (but without the anchovy fillets): https://kitchen-delights.blogspot.co.uk/2011/08/jamie-olivers-cauliflower-cheese.html